  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nLatest surface map shows the front draped just north of I-70. A\nlarge temperature range is in place over the forecast area early\nthis afternoon, ranging from the lower 70s near Galesburg, to\nthe mid 90s south of I-70. Convection has recently been firing\nsouth of I-70, along an axis of SBCAPE over 3000 J/kg, while\nscattered showers persist over the northern third of the\nforecast area. \n\nNot a lot of movement is expected with the front the next few\ndays, remaining draped over central Illinois courtesy of a large\nupper high over the southeast U.S. Gulf moisture has been \nfeeding into the mid Mississippi Valley due to the high's \ncirculation, and unseasonably high precipitable water values\nover 2 inches will remain pooled just north of the boundary. Two\nconsequences of this pattern: 1) Unseasonably hot weather with\nhighs in the 90s (mainly south of I-70) continuing into the\nweekend, and 2) potential for additional heavy rain across\nnorthern Illinois tonight. \n\n- Heat Concerns: A Heat Advisory has been issued for areas \n  south of I-70 through Thursday. Dew points in the lower 70s \n  will result in heat indices approaching 105 degrees both this \n  afternoon and Thursday afternoon. As the boundary slips a \n  little further south Thursday night, slightly lower \n  temperatures and dew points should keep the heat index just \n  under 100 degrees on Friday.\n\n- Rain Concerns: Morning model suite has been advertising development\n  of an MCS across southeast Iowa later this evening, tracking \n  eastward across northern Illinois overnight into early \n  Thursday. With the excessive precipitable water values and \n  training storms, potential for areas of flash flooding is \n  high. Heavy rain last night of 2-4 inches fell from the \n  southeast tip of Iowa to north of Peoria. While the core of \n  the heaviest rain tonight is expected closer to I-80, a number\n  of CAM's do show storms potentially tracking as far south as \n  I-74. HREF and REFS LPMM both suggest amounts around 2 inches \n  from Galesburg to Lacon are not out of the question. Thus, a \n  Flood Watch has been issued for Knox, Stark and Marshall \n  Counties until 10 am Thursday. \n\nGoing further out in time, while the front begins lifting back \nnorth as a warm front Friday night, the boundary never fully\nexits the region until early next week. At that point, a strong\nshortwave over the northern Plains and upper Mississippi Valley\nshould finally kick the front eastward. Until then, rain chances\nwill persist, with the highest chances from Saturday night into\nSunday night.",
